  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Low pressure south of the area will push a warm\nfront north through the region tonight through Wednesday.\n\nAfter a pleasant day (Tuesday), A warm front lifts north through\nthe region tonight, allowing showers and isolated thunderstorms\nto develop late tonight and into Wednesday morning. Low pressure\ndeveloping over the Southeast lifts north along the Eastern Seaboard,\nbut there is considerable uncertainty as to its track. \n\nThe overall system is weak with low pressure and upper short\nwave energy moving across our area Wed-Thu. The recent hot/humid\nairmass will return and even minor lifting will create the\npotential for more drenching downpours. Severe weather is not\nexpected with weak winds fields across the area. \n\nThe Weather Prediction Center has a Marginal Risk for Excessive\nRainfall, which is a 1 out of 4. for Wednesday and Thursday. For\nnow, will follow the NBM and carry high chance to low end likely\nPoPs for most of the region Wednesday. There is the potential \nfor another 1/2 inch to 1 inch of rain Wednesday afternoon and \nevening. The higher totals are presently forecasted to occur \nacross the southern Poconos and perhaps into the Lehigh Valley. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...For the later half of the week, we will have a \nhot, humid, and at times stormy pattern in place.\n\nExpect a prolonged period of southwesterly flow as our region \nwill be in between a Bermuda High well to our east, and a mid \nlevel long wave trough that will be slowly sliding east over the\nnorthern tier of the U.S./southern Canada. As such, heat and \nhumidity will be building throughout the later half of the week.\nAt this point, Friday and Saturday appear to be the warmest days,\nwith highs ranging from the low 80s (Poconos) to the low 90s\n(metro). That in and of itself is pretty close to normal. However,\nwith dewpoints in the mid 70s possible, we could get heat index\nvalues at or above 100 degrees for much of the area. All that being\nsaid, this still doesn't look nearly as impactful an extreme heat\nsetup as what we saw earlier this summer during the first week of\nJuly. \n\nThe other aspect of this are the daily storm chances, primarily confined\nto the afternoon and evening with a typical diurnal pattern. Given\nthe hot and humid conditions that will be in place, we can't rule\nout the more common thunderstorm hazards of damaging winds and heavy\nrain/flooding, but in general, I don't see any one day that has an\nespecially high threat of any of these at this time. If an embedded\nshort wave trough crosses our region as the larger trough axis approaches,\nthat could increase the risk, particularly for severe threats, but\ntrying to time that out at this point would be very difficult, given\nthat we are still seeing some differences between the models with\nthe larger pattern.",
